# Subagents

## Freedom of choice

Project agents in [`.cursor/agents/`](../agents/) are the **default starting point** — prefer them when they fit. The main agent is free to:

- Launch as many subagents as useful (including in parallel)
- Use project agents, other built-in/internal Task subagent types, or ad-hoc/custom subagents
- Skip project agents entirely when another approach is more effective
- Decide *whether*, *which*, and *when* to spawn — efficiency over ritual

Do not feel limited to the table below. Use judgment: if a project agent is a good match, use it; if not, use something else or none.

## Project agents (defaults)

On each user prompt, classify intent, then either handle alone or launch via the Task tool (`subagent_type` = agent `name` below when using a project agent). Prefer parallel launch when several concerns apply (e.g. feature + tests). Do **not** launch subagents for trivial one-file edits, typos, or pure Q&A.

| Agent | Role | Launch when |
|-------|------|-------------|
| **reviewer** | Read-only: correctness, security, quality, tests | After substantive code changes; before commit/PR; user asks for review / “проверь” / “review” |
| **tester** | Writes/updates tests only | New behavior or bugfix that needs coverage; can run **in parallel** while main agent implements |
| **architect** | Read-only: layers, boundaries, patterns | Layer-split / FE-shared-BE questions; large structural design; suspected circular deps or boundary violations |
| **refactorer** | Edits: quality-preserving refactors | Dedicated refactor / cleanup / DRY / rename-structure (not feature work) |
| **accessibility-checker** | a11y review/fixes | UI with forms, dialogs, buttons, images, keyboard/ARIA; user mentions a11y / WCAG / screen reader |
| **performance-analyzer** | Perf analysis/opts | Slow paths, lists/tables, N+1, heavy client renders, caching; user mentions perf / lag / optimize |

Efficiency:

- Match **intent**, not keywords alone (Russian/English both count).
- One clear specialist task → that subagent; mixed “build + review” → main implements, then **reviewer**.
- Do not spawn **reviewer** before there is something to review.
- Do not use **refactorer** for feature delivery or **architect** for line-level bugfixes.
- After launching, incorporate findings; for **reviewer**/**architect**, report findings and only edit if the user asked for fixes.
